text:
alex.k: Heads-up — onboarding sample app is hitting client-side stalls on the new JS SDK. Seems related to a cursor-lease not being released, causing a steady backlog and then soft-429s when the window fills.
maria: Ugh. Is this the new `leaseToken` API we added last week?
jamal: Yep. Saw similar in local repro. Sequence: fetch page -> acquire lease -> spawn async processing -> forget to release if processing errors out. Reservoir never frees slots.
sophie: Can someone paste the problematic snippet? I can probably whip up a minimal test. :mag_right:
alex.k: Here's the simplified pattern from the sample app (JS):
```js
const lease = await client.acquireLease(cursor);
processBatch(batch).then(() => {
  // missing await or catch -> lease.release() sometimes not hit
  lease.release();
});
// caller continues, assumes lease is released synchronously
```
maria: Ah — leaking promises. The SDK docs show `lease.release()` but don't stress transactional guarantees. Onboarding guide only showed the happy-path.
jamal: Two failure modes we saw: processing throws (no release) and processBatch uses `await` internally but rejects after some microtask scheduling so release runs too late/never. Also windowing logic in sample app expects release to be immediate which breaks concurrency accounting.
sophie: Proposed fixes: 1) make SDK release idempotent/safe to call multiple times, 2) update sample app to use try/finally with await, 3) telemetry for leaked leases so we can surface them in console.
alex.k: I'm in favor. Also we should add automatic guard in SDK: a finalizer that releases lease if GCed or after TTL. But careful with surprises for deterministic flows.
maria: For immediate mitigation, can we change sample app to a semaphore pattern that's robust to unhandled rejections? e.g.:
```js
await client.acquireLease(cursor);
try {
  await processBatch(batch);
} catch (e) {
  // log, rethrow
  throw e;
} finally {
  await client.releaseLease(cursor);
}
```
jamal: That will unblock onboarding folks. I'll open a PR to update the sample repo and docs.
sophie: SDK parity note: Python SDK already had `with client.lease(cursor):` context manager semantics. JS lacked an idiomatic `using`/`finally` example. We should include both patterns in the docs.
alex.k: Also noticed the retry layer in the SDK treats a 429 as retriable but doesn't decrement in-flight counters before retrying, so we end up with transient oversubscription. Need to ensure counters are adjusted on all retry paths.
maria: Good catch. That's definitely a concurrency accounting bug. Add a test that simulates rapid soft-429s while many leases are held.
ci-bot: CI: new test suite run queued for branch `fix/lease-leak` — will report status after run.
jamal: For the rollout plan: patch sample app + docs first (patch release), then a minor SDK patch to make release idempotent and ensure counters decrement on all exit paths. Finally, add a dashboard metric for "active leases" with alert when it drifts above expected for >5m.
sophie: Can we also add a small backpressure helper in the SDK to expose `maxConcurrent` and a builtin queue with timeouts? Would make it easier for customers to avoid this class of bug.
alex.k: Yep, but keep it opt-in. We don't want to hide the primitives. Add an example in the quickstart that wires the helper up to the paginator.
maria: I'll write the docs update and the quickstart snippet. Jamal, Sophie — can you pair on the SDK patch?
jamal: On it, pairing this afternoon.
ci-bot: CI: `fix/lease-leak` tests passed (e2e flaky: 1/23). :white_check_mark:
alex.k: Great, once CI green let's cut a patch and publish a short blurb in onboarding channel so new signups don't hit the old sample.
sophie: I'll follow up with a small postmortem note in docs explaining why `try/finally` is required here and link to the backpressure helper.
maria: Thanks team — this was causing confusing onboarding friction for new users.
alex.k: Filed follow-ups: PR#492 on sample app, PR#493 on JS SDK, DOCS#221 update quickstart. I'll tag the PRs in the channel when created. :rocket:
